# readme_basic_cu — what `basic.cu` is trying to accomplish

`test/mma/basic.cu` is a small CUDA program that is a **regression test for nixnan's tensor-core
(HMMA) instrumentation**. It deliberately drives one FP16 warp-level matrix multiply-accumulate
(WMMA) into each IEEE-754 exceptional condition, so nixnan has something known to detect. The
expected nixnan report is in `basic.expect` in the same directory.

`examples/basic.cu` is a byte-identical copy (checked with `diff`).

Build line (from the first comment in the file):

    nvcc -o main basic.cu -arch sm_86

## What the program computes

A single 16x16x16 tile, computed by one `wmma` sequence, in FP16 throughout:

    AB = A * B          (accumulator starts from fill_fragment 0)
    D  = A * B + C

`InitMatrix` sets A = B = identity and C = 0, so the clean answer is AB = I and D = I.
`run_test(A00, B00, C00, name)` then overwrites only element `[0,0]` of each matrix and
launches the kernel. Everything else stays 0 or 1, so a single special value is the only
disturbance. It prints the inputs (value and raw hex), runs the kernel, and prints `D[0,0]`.

`main()` runs six cases:

| # | A[0,0] | B[0,0] | C[0,0] | Intended exception | Native result for D[0,0] (RTX 3090, sm_86) |
|---|--------|--------|--------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------|
| 1 | 65504 (FP16 max normal) | 65504 | 0 | overflow: 65504 x 65504 -> +inf | `inf` (0x7c00) |
| 2 | +inf | 1 | -inf | NaN: inf + (-inf) | `nan` (0x7fff) |
| 3 | +min normal (0x0400) | 0.5 | 0 | underflow into the subnormal range | 0x0200, a subnormal (exact) |
| 4 | -min normal (0x8400) | 0.5 | 0 | same, negative | 0x8200, a subnormal |
| 5 | +min subnormal (0x0001) | 0.5 | 0 | underflow below the subnormals (half an ulp) | 0x0000, flushed to zero by rounding |
| 6 | -min subnormal (0x8001) | 0.5 | 0 | same, negative | 0x0000 (the sign is lost: -0 + C(+0) = +0) |

I compiled and ran the program natively (no nixnan) and the table's last column is that output.

## Code structure, briefly

- `hu` union plus `max_normal()`, `min_normal()`, `min_subnormal()` and the `neg_` forms build
  FP16 values from raw bit patterns (0x7BFF, 0x0400, 0x0001, sign bit 0x8000).
- `WMMAF16TensorCore` is the kernel. It uses `m16n16k16` FP16 fragments and two `mma_sync` calls,
  with `store_matrix_sync` for AB and for D. The compiler lowers each `mma_sync` to two
  `HMMA.16816.F16` SASS instructions, which is why this test exercises exactly the path nixnan
  instruments for tensor cores.
- `CalcWMMA` sets up the launch and has leftover timing code (its prints are commented out).
- Matrices live in CUDA managed (unified) memory, so the host fills them and reads D directly.

## Things worth knowing when reading it

- **Launch shape.** `blockDim = (128, 4)`, and the grid works out to (1, 1): one block of 512 threads
  = 16 warps. All 16 warps load the same tile and execute the same WMMA on the same data, so the
  work is redundant. For this test that is harmless, and it affects the nixnan counts (next point).
- **Why the counts in `basic.expect` look like multiples of 15.** FP16 NaN is 4 unique with 60
  repeats, Inf is 7 with 105, -Inf is 1 with 15, Subnormal is 6 with 186. 60 = 4 x 15 and
  105 = 7 x 15, 15 = 1 x 15. This fits 16 warps each reporting every exception, with the first
  report counted as "unique" and the other 15 as repeats. That is my reading of the numbers; I did
  not confirm it in the nixnan source. (186 is not a multiple of 15, so the subnormal count does not follow the
  same pattern; I have not worked out why.)
- **NaN spreads beyond [0,0].** In case 2, `A[0,0] = inf` multiplies `B[0,j] = 0` for the other
  columns in row 0, and inf x 0 = NaN. So whole row 0 of AB and D is NaN, not only element [0,0].
  This is probably why NaN has more than one unique location. Only `D[0,0]` is printed, and I did
  not dump the full matrix.
- **Memory-operation section.** The `FP16 Memory Operations: NaN 4 (60 repeats)` line in
  `basic.expect` is NaNs seen at the store instructions that write AB/D back to memory.
- **Type quirks.** `run_test` takes `half A00, half B00, float C00`; the float is narrowed to half when
  stored into `C[0]`. Calls pass `1.0`, `0.5`, `INFINITY` and `0.0`, which convert implicitly. The
  comment "AB = A*B + old(AB)" in the code is accurate only because `ab_frag` was zero-filled first.
- **No check of its own.** The program never asserts anything. It only prints. The pass/fail
  comes from running it under nixnan and comparing the report with `basic.expect`
  (the final `Exit Code: 0` line of that file belongs to the harness).
- The `.expect` file only covers FP16. The BF16, FP32 and FP64 sections are all zero, which makes
  the test also a check that nixnan does not misclassify HMMA operands as another type.

## Summary

`basic.cu` is a minimal, self-contained tensor-core exception generator: identity matrices with
one poisoned element, six FP16 cases covering overflow, invalid (NaN), and underflow to
subnormal and to zero, run as a single-tile WMMA on one 16-warp block. Its purpose is to show that nixnan
can see exceptions produced inside `HMMA` instructions, and to supply stable counts in
`basic.expect` for regression checking.
